  "body": "Fork synced at v0.18.0 (branch cut from upstream/main 7e3c75f9).\n\n## What changed\n\nThe holder law (#369) read a lathe body point at `|X + x|`, with `X` signed along the path's signed meridian (`SignedMeridian`). That frame takes its + side from the path's **first** off-axis point, which need not be the side the holder stands on. Take a rear tool that starts a hair past centre: every later tip is at X < 0 of that frame, and the body was read mirrored through the axis. A lip reaching the part was judged clear, and a clear body was judged to cross the axis.\n\n- New `crates/app/src/operation/turning_holder_side.rs`: `sided` gives the holder's side as a property of the whole path, read from its moves' meridian pieces. A rigid holder never flips mid-path, so the side is the one the path reaches farthest on. The other side may hold only a past-centre overrun of at most `PAST_CENTRE` = 2.5 mm, as in a face pass cleaning the centre pip, while the far side reaches beyond twice it. A path on both sides beyond that, or whose far side stays under 5 mm while the near side is off the axis, is refused by name: \"the lathe holder body cannot be judged: the path stands on both sides of the spindle axis ...\". A round XY arc about the axis (a thread's half-turn, whose ends alternate onto the antipode) tells no side: it is left out of the reading and read at |rho| on the holder's side.\n- `turning_holder::judge` reads `Reach::radii(side * X)`. On the + side the call is `radii(x)` exactly as before, so it is byte-identical. `body_axes`, which draws the body (#1038), flips its radial by the same side, so the drawn holder is still the judged one.\n- `camcraft-tools::lathe_holder`: the docs now state that X is signed along the holder's own +x, which is away from the axis on the holder's side, front or rear post alike.\n\n**Which signed X the generators produce.** Every turning generator places its straight moves' tips at `Frame::point(rho >= 0, ..)`, and a face pass runs to `rho = 0` at most. Threading is the exception: its half-turn arcs end on `Frame::antipode`, at -rho, by construction - so the side is read with those round arcs left out, and they are read at |rho| (as `kerf_of` already does). Every other generated tip is at X >= 0 of its own frame and reads as before. No turning op today carries a straight-move tip at X < 0, so the defect was latent and the issue stays P2, not re-rated P0. This is stated in the pinning tests and in `docs/decisions/1102-lathe-body-signed-reach.md`.\n\n**UI/MCP wording.** Main has no \"+x points away from the spindle axis\" text. That text is in #1043, and under this law it is now true as written. No wording change was needed here.\n\n**Expected merge with #1043 / #1061.** This PR touches only the reach reading in `turning_holder.rs` (a `side` field on `Scene`, one `sided_pieces` call in `judge` and one in `body_axes`, and the `radii` argument), plus a new module. Merging either way should be additive.\n\n## How tested\n\n- `operation::turning_holder_side::tests::the_side_is_where_the_path_reaches_and_a_past_centre_overrun_keeps_it`: front paths are +1, a face run 0.4 past centre keeps +1, and a rear path starting 0.5 past centre is -1. Both-sides paths are refused by name, and so are a near side off the axis with a far side under 5 mm (`[2, -3]`) and a non-finite radius.\n- `operation::turning_holder_side::tests::a_rear_tool_past_centre_is_read_on_its_own_side_like_its_front_mirror`: this runs `judge` end to end. A rear tool (tip at X -8) whose body lip reaches the part is convicted, and the same tool with an outward body passes. Both match the front mirror at X +8. A face start 0.4 past centre reads on the front side. A both-sides path is refused.\n- `operation::turning_holder_side::tests::a_thread_pass_winding_through_the_antipode_is_judged_not_refused`: a thread-kernel-shaped pass (six ccw half-turn arcs about the axis alternating +5.5/-5.5) judges `Ok` with an outward body and convicts a reaching one, front and rear mirror alike (the first cut refused it as both sides).\n- `operation::turning_holder_side::tests::the_drawn_body_flips_its_radial_with_the_holder_side`: `body_axes` returns the negated radial for a rear path, the frame's own for a front one, and draws a thread pass on its approach's side.\n- Mutation check: with the old `radii(x)` reading put back, the rear pin fails (`Ok([])` against `Ok([(1, true)])`), which is the missed strike.\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` on the final head 569f09b9 (upstream/main merged in, review findings fixed): green (app suites, the 130-suite gate map of `app::operation` plus `--lib`, and the ui suites; 0 failed, 9.8 min). `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-tools --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- A real rear-turret machine definition, because none exists. Also the GUI drawing of a flipped body (the `body_axes` flip is not pinned by its own test).\n- No golden or snapshot changes.\n\nChangelog: The lathe holder law reads a holder body on its own side of the spindle axis for a tip at either signed X, and refuses by name a path that stands on both sides (#1102)\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFixes #1102\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
